This will automatically register an instance of the RedisCache class as the concrete
implementation for any instances of IDistributedCache you inject into any of your
services. The localhost configuration setting will use the default configurations for a local
deployment of the Redis client, so there's no need to specify an IP address and port unless
you explicitly change it on your local deployment. Meanwhile, the InstanceName field
will give the entries stored in the cache that were set by this application an application-
specific prefix. So, in this example, if I set a record with the 1234 key with my setting of
local, that key will be stored in the cache as local1234. The RedisCache instance that is
registered by the AddDistributedRedisCache() method will automatically look for keys
with the InstanceName prefix that we've specified in our options. We'll see this later when
we inspect our cache instance.
With our Redis cache running, and our IDistributedCache instance configured and
registered with our dependency injection container, we can write a CacheService class.
This will follow a similar pattern to our DataService class, where it exposes only a small
